      "**Context**",
      "After the exchange with Professor Hu Jifan, we finally established circRNA as CirCure's core RNA expression platform. At this point, we could explain why we chose circRNA from the perspectives of molecular structure, stability, expression potential, and engineering space. But when we tried to introduce this technology to people outside the team, a new question emerged: can the public, for whom a technology deemed “reasonable” in the laboratory may look quite different, understand and accept it?",
      "For researchers, concepts such as circRNA, RNA vaccines, and synthetic biology have relatively clear scientific meanings; but for people without relevant professional backgrounds, words like “RNA,” “artificially synthesised,” “vaccine,” and “gene” may be understood in completely different cognitive frameworks. We realised that even if a technology has potential in the laboratory, if the public does not know what it is, why it is used, or has unanswered safety concerns, an important barrier remains between research outcomes and real-world application. We therefore decided to conduct an anonymous questionnaire survey among members of the public of different ages, educational backgrounds, and professional backgrounds, with Jilin University students and community residents as one of the main survey groups.",
      "**Issue**",
      "Unlike the earlier public cognition survey on breast cancer, this time our focus shifted from “disease cognition” to “technology cognition.” The questionnaire began with relatively basic synthetic biology concepts, asking whether the public had been exposed to synthetic biology and through which channels they usually learned about this field; it then gradually moved to RNA, RNA vaccines, and circRNA, hoping to observe respondents' familiarity with these concepts and the first impressions that different terms may evoke.",
      "In the questionnaire design, we did not tell respondents in advance that “circRNA is safe” or hint at any particular answer; Instead, we first assessed their existing knowledge and concerns, then provided basic information and observed how their understanding changed. In this way, we hoped to obtain not a simple “approval rate,” but a clearer map of public cognition: which concepts are understood, which are easily confused, and which questions truly block technology acceptance.",
      "**Reframing**",
      "At this stage, we hoped to use the survey to identify questions the project still had not answered well. If the public's understanding of synthetic biology and RNA technology differs markedly from that of researchers, this would mean that while CirCure continues to advance the Wet Lab, it must also seriously consider technology communication and social responsibility.",
      "Especially for a relatively novel technology such as circRNA, merely telling the public “it is more stable” or “it has expression advantages” is far from sufficient. What truly affects understanding and acceptance may be more specific questions: What happens after it enters the human body? Will it change people's genetic information? How long will it remain in the body? What if unexpected risks arise? Why use this new technology instead of existing treatments?",
      "These questions, in turn, helped us examine our own project. We began to realize that safety should not be merely an add-on explained to the public after the project is completed; It should be proactively considered during the technology development stage. Likewise, Education should not merely promote the project, but should take responsibility for helping the public distinguish scientific concepts, understand technology boundaries, and form reasonable risk perceptions.",
