      "**Context**",
      "After the public questionnaire, we visited Professor Niu Junqi with questions that had repeatedly appeared in public feedback. For researchers, statements such as “RNA does not enter the nucleus,” “there is no conventional genome integration process,” and “RNA is eventually metabolized by cells” seem like basic concepts in technical discussion; but in public communication, we found that words such as “synthetic RNA,” “gene,” “vaccine,” and “artificial design” are easily grouped together.",
      "Compared with what we initially hoped to explain to the public—“why circRNA has better stability and expression potential”—the questions respondents cared about were often more direct: Will it change my genes? How long will it remain after entering the body? Will it persist in the body for a long time? Could artificially synthesized RNA produce unpredictable effects? If a technology is still relatively new, how can we judge whether it is safe enough?",
      "These questions made us realize that what the public truly cares about is not whether the technology is “advanced,” but why we believe it deserves trust and whether we can clearly explain the technology's boundaries. We therefore did not rush to answer these questions with more popular-science content, but instead organized the typical questions and concerns collected in the questionnaire and visited Professor Niu Junqi with these genuine public responses.",
