      "We used the questions that repeatedly appeared in the public questionnaire as the starting point of the interview, asking him to help us judge: which questions can be explained on the basis of existing scientific knowledge, which still need to be supported by experimental data, and which questions should not be prematurely promised at the current stage of the project at all. We focused on several aspects.",
      "The first was the boundary between scientific facts and ethical statements. For example, the cytosolic expression characteristics and molecular structure of circRNA can help us explain its difference from DNA-level genetic modification, but this does not mean that we can simply equate “not entering the nucleus” or “no genomic integration” with “absolutely safe.” For any new biomedical technology, a safety judgment must be based on the specific molecular design, preparation quality, delivery system, dose, immune response, and long-term research data, rather than being generalized from a single technical feature.",
      "Second, we discussed the boundary between the research stage and clinical application. CirCure is still a project at the experimental research stage and has not yet conducted human trials. Therefore, in project introductions and public communication, we must clearly distinguish between “potential observed in experimental research” and “efficacy and safety already confirmed clinically,” and must not use expressions that could easily make the public believe the product can already be used to treat patients.",
      "Third, we brought the questions in the questionnaire about “long-term persistence,” “unknown side effects,” and “risk of loss of control” into the discussion. Professor Niu Junqi helped us recognize that, for these questions, the most responsible response is not simply to tell the public “it won't happen,” but to explain: which risks currently have relatively clear mechanistic bases, which still need to be verified experimentally, and through what means the team will continue to monitor and control these risks.",
