      "After the exchange with Professor Wang Yishu, we decided to adopt a multi-antigen, multi-epitope design, hoping to reduce dependence on a single target by combining different antigen sources. But once this direction was settled, we still did not know how to carry out antigen screening. At the beginning of the project, the team lacked experience in conducting dry-lab analyses independently. We knew we needed to find TNBC-related antigens, and we had reviewed research on some candidate targets, but we had not yet established a specific analytical approach. What data should be used, how to compare candidate antigens, and what judgments different results could support—all these questions needed to be sorted out. We therefore visited Associate Professor Guan Qingtian, introduced the project's disease target and multi-epitope vaccine concept, and hoped he could help us determine the starting point of the dry-lab work and build the subsequent analytical workflow.",
      "**Issue**",
      "During the exchange, we explained our current difficulties to Professor Guan. The literature could provide leads on candidate antigens, but we hoped to use public data to further judge whether these candidates were suitable to continue into vaccine design. Professor Guan suggested that we start with differential proteomics analysis of TNBC. Vaccine epitopes originate from proteins; Therefore, we first needed to examine the expression of candidate proteins in the target tumor tissue. By comparing protein abundance between TNBC and normal or adjacent tissues, we could learn whether the candidate protein can be detected and whether there are tumor-associated changes in abundance.",
      "This suggestion provided us with a concrete starting point. We began to search for proteomics datasets with clear TNBC sample information and corresponding controls, and learned how to organize sample groups, conduct differential analysis, and interpret the results. The judgment of candidate antigens was also grounded, from descriptions in the literature, in measurable protein data.",
      "On this basis, Professor Guan suggested that we add single-cell transcriptomic analysis. Proteomics measures protein abundance in tissue samples, but a tumor tissue simultaneously contains malignant cells, immune cells, and stromal cells. Therefore, even if a protein is abundant in tumor tissue, we still need to know in which cell populations the corresponding gene is mainly expressed. Single-cell data can help us compare gene expression across different cell populations, judge whether candidate genes are enriched in malignant cells, and observe their distribution in non-malignant cells. For multi-antigen design, we can also examine whether different candidate genes appear in different malignant cell populations, thereby assessing the complementarity of the combination.",
      "We also gradually understood the division of labor between these two types of data: proteomics provides evidence of protein abundance in tissue, while single-cell transcriptomics provides information on the cellular distribution of the corresponding gene—the latter helps explain where the expression signal is located, but cannot replace protein-level measurement.",
      "Subsequently, Professor Guan suggested that we use cBioPortal to further examine the distribution of candidate genes among TNBC patients. Looking only at cohort averages may overlook differences among patients. A candidate that is highly expressed in a few samples does not mean it will behave the same in other patients. Through patient-level queries, we could compare the distribution of each candidate gene under a unified rule and calculate, after combining multiple candidates, how many cases satisfy at least one of the rules. In this way, we could separately examine intra-tumor cellular differences and inter-patient differences, providing a basis for deciding whether to retain the multi-antigen combination.",
      "**Reframing**",
      "Professor Guan's suggestions helped us decompose the originally vague antigen screening task into analyses that could be carried out step by step: first examine candidate proteins in TNBC tissue, then examine the cellular distribution of the corresponding genes, and finally compare the patient-level distribution of candidate combinations.",
      "Following this approach, we used PXD024322 and PXD028102 for TNBC proteomics analysis, used GSE176078 to examine the cellular distribution of candidate genes, and queried TNBC cases in TCGA through cBioPortal. Existing literature and histopathological studies helped us further understand the disease relevance of these candidates and their expression in real tissues. Each of these analyses provides different evidence: higher protein abundance can support continuing to focus on an antigen source; Enrichment of a candidate gene in malignant cells helps determine its expression location; and different distributions of multiple candidates across cells and patients provide a basis for combination design.",
