      "We then moved on to industrial scale-up, focusing on common obstacles when translating laboratory processes into production: whether parameters remain applicable after scale-up, whether raw materials can be supplied reliably, whether equipment changes affect product quality, whether costs are manageable, whether consistency can be maintained between batches, and whether future regulatory requirements can be incorporated into development. These discussions reinforced that scale-up is not simply a proportional increase in experimental volume. A technology that performs well at a small scale may still encounter new challenges involving equipment, raw materials, parameter control, or quality consistency.",
      "We subsequently discussed pharmaceutical market positioning. Beyond efficacy, companies evaluating a new therapeutic product must clarify which patients it primarily serves, at what stage of the existing treatment pathway it would be used, what problems it addresses compared with available options, who would ultimately use and purchase it, and whether it would compete with, replace, or complement existing products. This helped us recognize that market positioning is not merely a presentation exercise undertaken after a project is completed; it should also inform which questions deserve priority during product development. Finally, we turned to the role of synthetic biology in the pharmaceutical industry. We asked whether, from an industry perspective, the decision to adopt a synthetic biology technology depends primarily on its novelty or on the practical advantages it can deliver. This question helped us reconsider the role of “synthetic biology” in CirCure: technological novelty is not an end in itself. Such innovation acquires industrial significance when it improves product performance, production efficiency, stability, cost, or clinical value.",
