      "We also specifically discussed the milestones a university team would usually need to reach before advancing a potential collaboration. This prompted us to reconsider project development in terms of milestones. Rather than broadly stating that we would “continue optimizing CirCure,” we needed to ask more practical questions: which data would demonstrate that the project had advanced beyond its current stage? Which key technical risks would need to be resolved before a company could reassess it? Which process development, quality, or validation activities would be important prerequisites for further industry discussions? Finally, we asked how an early-stage project could establish a realistic industrialization pathway without prematurely setting “starting a business” or “bringing a product to market” as its goal. This discussion helped us understand that Entrepreneurship in an early-stage project is not about announcing commercial success in advance. More importantly, it involves progressively reducing uncertainty for the next stage: first demonstrate technology, then fill critical evidence gaps, establish manufacturability and quality systems, and seek appropriate industry resources and forms of collaboration as the project matures.",
